# description : This .audit is designed against the CIS SUSE Linux Enterprise Server 15 STIG Benchmark v1.0.0
#
#<ui_metadata>
#<display_name>CIS SUSE Linux Enterprise Server 15 STIG v1.0.0 CAT I</display_name>
#<spec>
#  <type>CIS</type>
#  <name>SUSE Linux Enterprise Server 15 STIG</name>
#  <profile>CAT I</profile>
#  <version>1.0.0</version>
#  <link>https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/23779</link>
#</spec>
#<labels>agent,suse_linux_15</labels>
#<benchmark_refs>800-53,800-53r5,CAT,CCI,LEVEL,Rule-ID,STIG-ID,Vuln-ID</benchmark_refs>
#<variables>
#  <variable>
#    <name>PLATFORM_VERSION</name>
#    <default>15</default>
#    <description>SUSE Version</description>
#    <info>SUSE Version</info>
#    <value_type>STRING</value_type>
#  </variable>
#</variables>
#</ui_metadata>

<check_type:"Unix">

<if>
  <condition type:"AND">
    <custom_item>
      type        : FILE_CONTENT_CHECK
      description : "SUSE Linux 15 is installed - VERSION"
      file        : "/etc/os-release"
      regex       : "^[\\s]*VERSION[\\s]*="
      expect      : "^[\\s]*VERSION[\\s]*=\"[\\s]*@PLATFORM_VERSION@"
    </custom_item>

    <custom_item>
      type        : FILE_CONTENT_CHECK
      description : "SUSE Linux 15 is installed - NAME"
      file        : "/etc/os-release"
      regex       : "^[\\s]*NAME[\\s]*="
      expect      : "^[\\s]*NAME[\\s]*=\"[\\s]*SLES[\\s]*\"$"
    </custom_item>
  </condition>

  <then>
    <report type:"PASSED">
      description : "CIS_SUSE_Linux_Enterprise_Server_15_STIG_v1.0.0_CAT_I.audit from CIS SUSE Linux Enterprise Server 15 STIG v1.0.0"
      see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/23779"
      show_output : YES
    </report>

    <custom_item>
      type        : FILE_CONTENT_CHECK
      description : "1.1 SLES-15-010000"
      info        : "The SUSE operating system must be a vendor-supported release.

GROUP ID: V-234800
RULE ID: SV-234800r991589

A SUSE operating system release is considered \"supported\" if the vendor continues to provide security patches for the product. With an unsupported release, it will not be possible to resolve security issues discovered in the system software."
      solution    : "Upgrade the SUSE operating system to a version supported by the vendor. If the system is not registered with the SUSE Customer Center, register the system against the correct subscription.

If the system requires Long-Term Service Pack Support (LTSS), obtain the correct LTSS subscription for the system."
      reference   : "800-171|3.14.1,800-171r3|03.14.01,800-171r3|03.14.01b.,800-53|SI-2c.,800-53|SI-2d.,800-53r5|SI-2d.,CAT|I,CCI|CCI-001230,CN-L3|8.1.4.4(e),CN-L3|8.1.10.5(a),CN-L3|8.1.10.5(b),CN-L3|8.5.4.1(b),CN-L3|8.5.4.1(d),CN-L3|8.5.4.1(e),CSF|ID.RA-1,CSF|PR.IP-12,CSF2.0|ID.IM-01,CSF2.0|ID.IM-02,CSF2.0|ID.IM-03,CSF2.0|PR.PS-02,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ISO-27001-2022|A.6.8,ISO-27001-2022|A.8.8,ISO-27001-2022|A.8.32,ITSG-33|SI-2,ITSG-33|SI-2c.,LEVEL|1M,NESA|T7.6.2,NESA|T7.7.1,NIAv2|AM38,NIAv2|AM39,NIAv2|PR9,NIAv2|SS14b,PCI-DSSv3.2.1|6.2,PCI-DSSv4.0|6.3,PCI-DSSv4.0|6.3.3,QCSC-v1|11.2,Rule-ID|SV-234800r991589_rule,STIG-ID|SLES-15-010000,SWIFT-CSCv1|2.2,Vuln-ID|V-234800"
      see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/23779"
      file        : "/etc/os-release"
      regex       : "^[\\s]*VERSION[\\s]*="
      expect      : "^[\\s]*VERSION[\\s]*=\"[\\s]*15(\\.\\d+)?(-SP\\d+)?[\\s]*\"$"
    </custom_item>

    <custom_item>
      type        : FILE_CONTENT_CHECK_NOT
      description : "1.102 SLES-15-020300"
      info        : "The SUSE operating system must not be configured to allow blank or null passwords.

GROUP ID: V-234898
RULE ID: SV-234898r991589

Passwords need to be protected at all times, and encryption is the standard method for protecting passwords. If passwords are not encrypted, they can be plainly read (i.e., clear text) and easily compromised."
      solution    : "Configure the SUSE operating system to not allow blank or null passwords.

Remove any instances of the \"nullok\" option in \"/etc/pam.d/common-auth\" and \"/etc/pam.d/common-password\" to prevent logons with empty passwords."
      reference   : "800-171|3.4.2,800-171r3|03.04.02a.,800-53|CM-6b.,800-53r5|CM-6b.,CAT|I,CCI|CCI-000366,CN-L3|8.1.10.6(d),CSF|PR.IP-1,CSF2.0|DE.CM-09,CSF2.0|PR.PS-01,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ISO-27001-2022|A.8.9,ITSG-33|CM-6b.,LEVEL|1M,NESA|T3.2.1,Rule-ID|SV-234898r991589_rule,STIG-ID|SLES-15-020300,SWIFT-CSCv1|2.3,Vuln-ID|V-234898"
      see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/23779"
      file        : "/etc/pam.d/*"
      regex       : "^[\\s]*[^#\\n\\r]+nullok"
      expect      : "^[\\s]*[^#\\n\\r]+nullok"
    </custom_item>

    <custom_item>
      type        : FIND_CMD
      description : "1.168 SLES-15-040020"
      info        : "There must be no .shosts files on the SUSE operating system.

GROUP ID: V-234984
RULE ID: SV-234984r991589

The .shosts files are used to configure host-based authentication for individual users or the system via SSH. Host-based authentication is not sufficient for preventing unauthorized access to the system, as it does not require interactive identification and authentication of a connection request, or for the use of two-factor authentication."
      solution    : "Remove any \".shosts\" files found on the SUSE operating system.

sudo rm /[path]/[to]/[file]/.shosts"
      reference   : "800-171|3.4.2,800-171r3|03.04.02a.,800-53|CM-6b.,800-53r5|CM-6b.,CAT|I,CCI|CCI-000366,CN-L3|8.1.10.6(d),CSF|PR.IP-1,CSF2.0|DE.CM-09,CSF2.0|PR.PS-01,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ISO-27001-2022|A.8.9,ITSG-33|CM-6b.,LEVEL|1M,NESA|T3.2.1,Rule-ID|SV-234984r991589_rule,STIG-ID|SLES-15-040020,SWIFT-CSCv1|2.3,Vuln-ID|V-234984"
      see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/23779"
      not_expect  : ".+"
      find_name   : ".shosts"
      find_type   : "f"
    </custom_item>

    <custom_item>
      type        : FIND_CMD
      description : "1.169 SLES-15-040030"
      info        : "There must be no shosts.equiv files on the SUSE operating system.

GROUP ID: V-234985
RULE ID: SV-234985r991589

The shosts.equiv files are used to configure host-based authentication for the system via SSH. Host-based authentication is not sufficient for preventing unauthorized access to the system, as it does not require interactive identification and authentication of a connection request, or for the use of two-factor authentication."
      solution    : "Remove any \"shosts.equiv\" files found on the SUSE operating system.

sudo rm /[path]/[to]/[file]/shosts.equiv"
      reference   : "800-171|3.4.2,800-171r3|03.04.02a.,800-53|CM-6b.,800-53r5|CM-6b.,CAT|I,CCI|CCI-000366,CN-L3|8.1.10.6(d),CSF|PR.IP-1,CSF2.0|DE.CM-09,CSF2.0|PR.PS-01,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ISO-27001-2022|A.8.9,ITSG-33|CM-6b.,LEVEL|1M,NESA|T3.2.1,Rule-ID|SV-234985r991589_rule,STIG-ID|SLES-15-040030,SWIFT-CSCv1|2.3,Vuln-ID|V-234985"
      see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/23779"
      not_expect  : ".+"
      find_name   : "shosts.equiv"
      find_type   : "f"
    </custom_item>

    <custom_item>
      type        : CMD_EXEC
      description : "1.172 SLES-15-040060"
      info        : "The SUSE operating system must disable the x86 Ctrl-Alt-Delete key sequence.

GROUP ID: V-234988
RULE ID: SV-234988r991589

A locally logged-on user, who presses Ctrl-Alt-Delete when at the console, can reboot the system. If accidentally pressed, as could happen in the case of a mixed OS environment, this can create the risk of short-term loss of availability of systems due to unintentional reboot. In the graphical user interface environment, risk of unintentional reboot from the Ctrl-Alt-Delete sequence is reduced because the user will be prompted before any action is taken."
      solution    : "Configure the system to disable the Ctrl-Alt-Delete sequence for the command line with the following commands:

sudo systemctl disable ctrl-alt-del.target sudo systemctl mask ctrl-alt-del.target

And reload the daemon to take effect

sudo systemctl daemon-reload"
      reference   : "800-171|3.4.2,800-171r3|03.04.02a.,800-53|CM-6b.,800-53r5|CM-6b.,CAT|I,CCI|CCI-000366,CN-L3|8.1.10.6(d),CSF|PR.IP-1,CSF2.0|DE.CM-09,CSF2.0|PR.PS-01,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ISO-27001-2022|A.8.9,ITSG-33|CM-6b.,LEVEL|1M,NESA|T3.2.1,Rule-ID|SV-234988r991589_rule,STIG-ID|SLES-15-040060,SWIFT-CSCv1|2.3,Vuln-ID|V-234988"
      see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/23779"
      cmd         : "/bin/systemctl is-enabled ctrl-alt-del.target | /usr/bin/awk '{print} END {if(NR==0) print \"disabled\" }'"
      expect      : "^masked$"
    </custom_item>

    <if>
      <condition type:"AND">
        <custom_item>
          type        : RPM_CHECK
          description : "Check GDM installed"
          rpm         : "gdm-0.0.0-0"
          operator    : "gt"
          required    : YES
        </custom_item>
      </condition>

      <then>
        <if>
          <condition auto:"FAILED" type:"AND">
            <custom_item>
              type        : CMD_EXEC
              description : "logout get"
              cmd         : "/usr/bin/gsettings get org.gnome.settings-daemon.plugins.media-keys logout"
              expect      : "^''$"
            </custom_item>

            <custom_item>
              type        : CMD_EXEC
              description : "logout writable"
              cmd         : "/usr/bin/gsettings writable org.gnome.settings-daemon.plugins.media-keys logout"
              expect      : "^false$"
            </custom_item>
          </condition>

          <then>
            <report type:"PASSED">
              description : "1.173 SLES-15-040061"
              info        : "The SUSE operating system must disable the x86 Ctrl-Alt-Delete key sequence for Graphical User Interfaces.

GROUP ID: V-234989
RULE ID: SV-234989r991589

A locally logged-on user, who presses Ctrl-Alt-Delete when at the console, can reboot the system. If accidentally pressed, as could happen in the case of a mixed OS environment, this can create the risk of short-term loss of availability of systems due to unintentional reboot. In the graphical user interface environment, risk of unintentional reboot from the Ctrl-Alt-Delete sequence is reduced because the user will be prompted before any action is taken."
              solution    : "Configure the system to disable the Ctrl-Alt-Delete sequence for thegraphical user interface.

Create a database to contain the system-wide setting (if it does notalready exist) with the following steps:

 - Create a user profile and with the listed content:

/etc/dconf/profile/user
user-db:user
system-db:local
 - Create the following directories:

sudo mkdir -p /etc/dconf/db/local.d/
sudo mkdir -p /etc/dconf/db/local.d/locks/
 - Add the following files with the listed content:

/etc/dconf/db/local.d/01-fips-settings
[org/gnome/settings-daemon/plugins/media-keys]
logout=['']

/etc/dconf/db/local.d/locks/01-fips-locks
/org/gnome/settings-daemon/plugins/media-keys/logout
 - Update the dconf database:

sudo dconf update"
              reference   : "800-171|3.4.2,800-171r3|03.04.02a.,800-53|CM-6b.,800-53r5|CM-6b.,CAT|I,CCI|CCI-000366,CN-L3|8.1.10.6(d),CSF|PR.IP-1,CSF2.0|DE.CM-09,CSF2.0|PR.PS-01,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ISO-27001-2022|A.8.9,ITSG-33|CM-6b.,LEVEL|1M,NESA|T3.2.1,Rule-ID|SV-234989r991589_rule,STIG-ID|SLES-15-040061,SWIFT-CSCv1|2.3,Vuln-ID|V-234989"
              see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/23779"
              show_output : YES
            </report>
          </then>
        </if>
      </then>

      <else>
        <report type:"PASSED">
          description : "1.173 SLES-15-040061"
          info        : "The SUSE operating system must disable the x86 Ctrl-Alt-Delete key sequence for Graphical User Interfaces.

GROUP ID: V-234989
RULE ID: SV-234989r991589

A locally logged-on user, who presses Ctrl-Alt-Delete when at the console, can reboot the system. If accidentally pressed, as could happen in the case of a mixed OS environment, this can create the risk of short-term loss of availability of systems due to unintentional reboot. In the graphical user interface environment, risk of unintentional reboot from the Ctrl-Alt-Delete sequence is reduced because the user will be prompted before any action is taken."
          solution    : "Configure the system to disable the Ctrl-Alt-Delete sequence for thegraphical user interface.

Create a database to contain the system-wide setting (if it does notalready exist) with the following steps:

 - Create a user profile and with the listed content:

/etc/dconf/profile/user
user-db:user
system-db:local
 - Create the following directories:

sudo mkdir -p /etc/dconf/db/local.d/
sudo mkdir -p /etc/dconf/db/local.d/locks/
 - Add the following files with the listed content:

/etc/dconf/db/local.d/01-fips-settings
[org/gnome/settings-daemon/plugins/media-keys]
logout=['']

/etc/dconf/db/local.d/locks/01-fips-locks
/org/gnome/settings-daemon/plugins/media-keys/logout
 - Update the dconf database:

sudo dconf update"
          reference   : "800-171|3.4.2,800-171r3|03.04.02a.,800-53|CM-6b.,800-53r5|CM-6b.,CAT|I,CCI|CCI-000366,CN-L3|8.1.10.6(d),CSF|PR.IP-1,CSF2.0|DE.CM-09,CSF2.0|PR.PS-01,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ISO-27001-2022|A.8.9,ITSG-33|CM-6b.,LEVEL|1M,NESA|T3.2.1,Rule-ID|SV-234989r991589_rule,STIG-ID|SLES-15-040061,SWIFT-CSCv1|2.3,Vuln-ID|V-234989"
          see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/23779"
